          "stimulus": null,
          "question": "Every ten years following the census, state legislative majorities redraw congressional district boundaries. By 'packing' opposition voters into a single district or 'cracking' them across multiple districts, the party in power secures an electoral majority without gaining additional raw votes. In geographic spatial theory, this political practice represents an applied real-world manipulation of:",
